==English==
===Etymology===
{{rfc}}

[[bench]] (from 19th century land surveying jargon) + [[mark]]: Benchmark originally referred to a mark cut into a stone or a wall by surveyors measuring a tract of land. The cut was used to secure a bracket called a "bench" upon which measuring equipment was mounted. All subsequent measurements were made in reference to the position and height of that mark. The surveying term is first recorded in 1842, and the figurative use arose by 1884.<ref>[http://www.etymologie.info/~e/u_/us-manage.html#Benchmark Benchmark etymology on etymologie.info]</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# a [[standard]] by which something is [[evaluated]] or [[measured]]
# a [[surveyor]]'s [[mark]] made on some [[stationary]] [[object]] and shown on a [[map]]; used as a [[reference]] [[point]]

